FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] `tests/bench/mod.rs:658` — `parse_baseline_markdown` skips any pipe-line whose cell count is not exactly 4 *before* it checks whether the first cell names a known workload, so a corrupted known row like `| fibonacci | 20 | 6765 |` (missing the wall-time cell) is silently dropped. The function's own contract at `tests/bench/mod.rs:644-648` says "a corrupted baseline cannot compare as if it had fewer workloads", and `comparison_table` (`tests/bench/mod.rs:768-797`) then runs only the rows that survived and the CLI exits 0. A user pointing `rb-bench --compare` at a truncated/edited baseline gets a clean success that silently omits a workload. Fix: before the `cells.len() != 4 { continue; }`, check `cells.first()` against `workload_names()` and return an error when a known workload appears in a malformed row; add a test with a 3-cell and a 5-cell known-workload row.

2. [MAJOR] `tests/bench_test.rs:38` — the "records hardware" assertion is `text.to_lowercase().contains("hardware")`, but `render_markdown` always writes the prose sentence "The hardware line is best-effort; ...", which itself contains the word "hardware" (`tests/bench/mod.rs:448-449`). Deleting the entire `## Hardware` section and the CPU line from `BENCH.md` would still pass this test, so the checked-in baseline's hardware note is not actually pinned despite the REPORT claiming the test "requires ... the hardware note". The same weak check appears at `tests/bench_test.rs:470`. Fix: assert on `text.contains("## Hardware")` and `text.contains("CPU:")` from the checked-in file (the synthetic-render test at `tests/bench_test.rs:903` already shows how to pin the value).

3. [MAJOR] `tests/bench/mod.rs:773` — the new `--compare`/baseline refusal branches have no tests: the empty-baseline error (`mod.rs:773-778`), the missing/unreadable-baseline error (`mod.rs:769-770`), the wrong-arity error (`mod.rs:840-845`), and the negative-size baseline row (`mod.rs:677-682`) are all unreachable from the suite. `edge_cli_compare_reruns_every_baseline_row` (`tests/bench_test.rs:692`) only exercises the happy path. This is a new feature added in this round, so the refusal channels need their own fixtures (missing file, empty file, `["--compare"]`, `["--compare","a","b"]`, `| fibonacci | -1 | 0 | 1.000 ms |`).

4. [MINOR] `tests/bench/mod.rs:705` — `parse_baseline_markdown` stores `answer: cells[2].to_string()` without validating it, and `comparison_table` re-runs the workload but never compares the re-run answer against the baseline's recorded answer (`tests/bench/mod.rs:791-793`). A baseline whose answer column is wrong or empty still yields exit 0 and a comparison table, even though the file's header calls the column "verified answers". Fix either by comparing `measurement.answer.to_string()` to `row.answer` (and refusing on mismatch) or by documenting that answers in an externally supplied baseline are ignored; add a test with a hand-corrupted answer.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
